 in that we now know  what we're probably going to encounter  from our suppliers to be honest answers  from things blowing you and so that's  what the violence we kind of get one  step ahead of our problem we get the  preventive the Bible trolls that can  take care of your ribs our spider mite  and we're ready for them when they  arrive so they don't build up the  population and we don't have to ni p.m.  yes you may have a little bit of an  explosion of a population in a corner  we're just treat the corner I don't go  through we don't wipe out the whole  greenhouse in a spray program we're very  very conservative in what we apply  and the biggest thing with I p.m. is  that it's a group effort when we first  were introduced to the whole concept of  biologicals into our IPM program we were  introduced to a wonderful grower over in  Buffalo at millers mark Aidan and he  just said call me up if you have  questions just let me know I'll gladly  help you through it and I did and I  called with very simple questions  sometimes I was almost embarrassed  because I felt I was so naive and Mark  would just say no that's right just do  it and we did and i have to say along  the way we stumbled a lot we got very  discouraged but we just kept going ahead  and if it wasn't for the ipm staff that  really helped guide us through a lot of  the problems we encounter because once I  p.m. you have to know what you're trying  to control they helped us with the  diagnosis and understanding the life  cycle about the diseases and the insects  and together it was just a wonderful  experience  it's been great because you know what  our customers are more knowledgeable we  have these wonderful posters that we put  together to explain to them what I p.m.  is and what we're doing and when they  see brand on their plans and what it  means and every single person that  stands there and reads it we know we've  just helped further the education of ipm  and and expanding it so that they know  there are other options for them in  their home garden as well as growers and  they can be more discerning about what  they purchase it's also been great  because our employees all know now at  depth Scouts but when we're full in May  we can't rely just on den we try to  teach we teach them all that you all  have to be looking you all have to be  noticing what's on that plant and if it  looks different what to do  when we explain it to just a home  gardener homeowner who really has no  biology yes you can see the like oh oh  again and if it helps too I mean the  little spots on your leaves of your kale  that you're buying it's okay that's  really what it's all about if there's a  blemish or if they're yeah it's not a  perfect green the customer now goes but  it hasn't been sprayed with anything  that's going to be toxic to me or my kid  you know we have little kids and they do  the tasting tour through the greenhouse  they taste the tomatoes they tasted the  stevia they taste that strawberries and  they can go through a key  this has been a production of Cornell  University on the web at cornell edu
 

